# Command psql

## What does this command do:

Interactive PostgreSQL terminal client for running queries and managing databases

## Installed from

For Fedora install using: sudo dnf install postgresql.x86_64
For Raspberry Pi install using: sudo apt update && sudo apt install postgresql-client
For Mac install using: brew update && brew install libpq && brew link --force libpq
For Mac container (Fedora Linux, ARM/aarch64) install using: sudo dnf install postgresql.aarch64

## Command path

For Fedora run: /usr/bin/psql
For Raspberry Pi run: /usr/bin/psql
For Mac run: /opt/homebrew/opt/libpq/bin/psql
For Mac container run: /usr/bin/psql

## Command documentation

Docs can be found at https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-psql.html

## Command options

# Connect to a database
psql -h HOSTNAME -p PORT -U USERNAME -d DBNAME

# Run a single SQL command and exit
psql -c "SELECT version();" -d DBNAME

# Execute commands from a file
psql -f script.sql -d DBNAME

# List available databases
psql -l

# Connect (interactive) — prompts for password
psql -h HOSTNAME -U USERNAME -W DBNAME

# Output as CSV
psql --csv -c "SELECT * FROM table;" -d DBNAME

# Expanded (vertical) output
psql -x -c "SELECT * FROM table;" -d DBNAME

# Tuples only (no headers/footers)
psql -t -c "SELECT count(*) FROM table;" -d DBNAME

# Send session log to file
psql -L session.log -d DBNAME

# No-align output with custom separator
psql -A -F ',' -c "SELECT * FROM table;" -d DBNAME

# Run as single transaction (non-interactive)
psql -1 -f script.sql -d DBNAME

# Set a psql variable
psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -f script.sql -d DBNAME

# Inside psql: list tables
\dt

# Inside psql: describe table
\d tablename

# Inside psql: list all schemas
\dn

# Inside psql: quit
\q
